WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.664
hello-jvm has two projects.

2
00:00:04.664 --> 00:00:07.501
greeting is a library, and app uses it.

3
00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:09.611
Here are some of their tasks.

4
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:15.690
Day 28's up-to-date check only helps when the outputs are still there, in the same folder.

5
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.033
Run clean, and the outputs are gone.

6
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.051
Every task does its work again.

7
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:25.475
Now turn on the build cache, with one line in gradle.properties.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:30.869
Before a cacheable task runs, Gradle hashes all its inputs into one cache key.

9
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.605
It runs the task, and stores the outputs under that key.

10
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:39.213
The same happens for each cacheable task: compiling, and testing.

11
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.818
Now run clean build again.

12
00:00:41.818 --> 00:00:43.502
Clean deletes the outputs.

13
00:00:43.502 --> 00:00:47.129
But the inputs did not change, so the keys are the same.

14
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:52.772
Gradle finds each key in the cache, copies the outputs in, and prints FROM-CACHE.

15
00:00:52.772 --> 00:00:54.920
Compiling and testing never run.

16
00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:59.815
The jars are rebuilt, because copying files is cheaper than caching them.

17
00:00:59.815 --> 00:01:01.419
Seven seconds became one.

18
00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:04.517
The folder path is not part of the key.

19
00:01:04.517 --> 00:01:07.764
So a fresh clone, in another folder, gets hits too.

20
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:15.184
A fresh clone of hello-jvm on the same laptop built with eight tasks executed, and twelve from cache.

21
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:21.354
Many teams also run a remote cache, an HTTP server every build can read.

22
00:01:21.354 --> 00:01:27.484
The Gradle docs recommend that CI fills it from clean builds, and laptops only read.

23
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:31.333
Pull main in the morning, and most of it is already compiled.

24
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:35.822
The Kotlin plugin adds one more trick, compile avoidance.

25
00:01:35.822 --> 00:01:38.708
Change greeting in three ways, and watch app.

26
00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:39.216
One.

27
00:01:39.216 --> 00:01:40.207
Add a comment.

28
00:01:40.207 --> 00:01:42.685
Only greeting's compileKotlin runs.

29
00:01:42.685 --> 00:01:48.560
It produces the same class files, so the jar and everything in app stay UP-TO-DATE.

30
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:49.660
Two.

31
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.346
Change a function body.

32
00:01:51.346 --> 00:01:54.644
Now the greeting jar and both test tasks run.

33
00:01:54.644 --> 00:01:57.649
But app's compileKotlin stays UP-TO-DATE.

34
00:01:57.867 --> 00:01:59.436
The ABI had not changed.

35
00:01:59.436 --> 00:02:03.555
That is the public signatures that other code compiles against.

36
00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:04.840
Three.

37
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.573
Add a public function.

38
00:02:06.573 --> 00:02:10.829
The ABI changes, and now app's compileKotlin runs too.

39
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:15.767
So a change behind a stable public API does not ripple through the build.

40
00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:18.620
One more reason to split code into projects.

41
00:02:19.533 --> 00:02:21.794
Gradle has four ways to skip work.

42
00:02:21.794 --> 00:02:24.320
Up-to-date checks, in the same folder.

43
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.577
The build cache, in any folder or on any machine.

44
00:02:27.577 --> 00:02:31.233
The configuration cache, which skips the build scripts.

45
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:33.028
And the daemon, a warm JVM.
